1
00:04:41,538 --> 00:04:47,538
Anyone is an enemy for a price.

2
00:09:23,076 --> 00:09:29,076
Ronin is a action, thriller, crime film directed by John Frankenheimer.

3
00:14:04,615 --> 00:14:10,615
A briefcase with undisclosed contents – sought by Irish terrorists and
the Russian mob – makes its way into criminals' hands.

4
00:18:46,153 --> 00:18:52,153
An Irish liaison assembles a squad of mercenaries, or 'ronin', and
gives them the thorny task of recovering the case.

5
00:23:27,692 --> 00:23:33,692
Sam, played by Robert De Niro.

6
00:28:09,230 --> 00:28:15,230
Vincent, played by Jean Reno.

7
00:32:50,769 --> 00:32:56,769
Deirdre, played by Natascha McElhone.

8
00:37:32,307 --> 00:37:38,307
Gregor, played by Stellan Skarsgård.

9
00:42:13,846 --> 00:42:19,846
Larry, played by Skipp Sudduth.

10
00:46:55,384 --> 00:47:01,384
Seamus O'Rourke, played by Jonathan Pryce.

11
00:51:36,923 --> 00:51:42,923
Ronin is a 1998 American  neo-noir action thriller film directed by John Frankenheimer and written
by John David Zeik and David Mamet, the latter under the pseudonym Richard Weisz.

12
00:56:18,461 --> 00:56:24,461
It stars an ensemble cast consisting of Robert De Niro, Jean Reno, Natascha
McElhone, Stellan Skarsgård, Sean Bean and Jonathan Pryce.

13
01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:06,000
The film is about a team of former special operatives who are hired to steal a mysterious,
heavily guarded briefcase while navigating a maze of shifting loyalties.

14
01:05:41,538 --> 01:05:47,538
The film was praised for its realistic car chases in Nice and Paris.

15
01:10:23,076 --> 01:10:29,076
Frankenheimer signed to direct Zeik's screenplay, which Mamet rewrote in
1997 to expand De Niro's role and develop plot details.

16
01:15:04,615 --> 01:15:10,615
The film was photographed by Robert Fraisse in his native
France from November 3, 1997, to March 3, 1998.

17
01:19:46,153 --> 01:19:52,153
Professional racing car drivers coordinated and performed the vehicle stunts,
and Elia Cmiral scored the film, his first for a major studio.

18
01:24:27,692 --> 01:24:33,692
Ronin premiered at the 1998 Venice Film Festival before
its general release on September 25.

19
01:29:09,230 --> 01:29:15,230
Critics were generally positive about the film's action, casting and
technical aspects, while the plot attracted criticism.

20
01:33:50,769 --> 01:33:56,769
The film underperformed at the box office, grossing
$70.7 million on a $55 million budget.

21
01:38:32,307 --> 01:38:38,307
Ronin, Frankenheimer's last well-received feature film, was
considered to be a return to form for the director.

22
01:43:13,846 --> 01:43:19,846
Film critic and historian Stephen Prince called the film
Frankenheimer's "end-of-career masterpiece".

23
01:47:55,384 --> 01:48:01,384
The car chases, which were favorably compared with those in Bullitt and The French Connection,
were included on several media outlets' lists as among the best depicted on film.

24
01:52:36,923 --> 01:52:42,923
The film explores themes of france, paris, france,
arms deal, mercenary, suitcase.

25
01:57:18,461 --> 01:57:24,461
The tone is intense.
